1
00:00:00,773 --> 00:00:04,273
(We Have Become a Family)

2
00:00:08,633 --> 00:00:10,033
They'll see what real running is all about now.

3
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:12,103
Gosh, it is different.

4
00:00:12,303 --> 00:00:14,242
He's starting on an uphill to make our run look like nothing.

5
00:00:14,243 --> 00:00:15,243
Right.

6
00:00:15,403 --> 00:00:16,613
It's so annoying.

7
00:00:19,143 --> 00:00:22,142
(He heads somewhere after a proper run.)

8
00:00:22,143 --> 00:00:23,183
Gosh, I'm tired.

9
00:00:25,183 --> 00:00:26,682
(Ringing)

10
00:00:26,683 --> 00:00:27,883
Where is this place?

11
00:00:30,753 --> 00:00:32,822
(Barking)

12
00:00:32,823 --> 00:00:33,863
- Food. - Hey.

13
00:00:34,063 --> 00:00:35,963
- Your brother is back from his run. - Come here. Hey.

14
00:00:36,333 --> 00:00:37,563
Your brother came back from his exercise.

15
00:00:37,593 --> 00:00:39,263
Come here.

16
00:00:39,733 --> 00:00:40,932
- My goodness. - Come here.

17
00:00:40,933 --> 00:00:42,603
- Oh, my. They're so cute! - Come back here.

18
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:46,203
- It's your brother. - They're Alkong and Dalkong.

19
00:00:47,273 --> 00:00:48,342
Come here.

20
00:00:48,343 --> 00:00:49,613
It's your brother.

21
00:00:49,913 --> 00:00:50,973
Dalkong.

22
00:00:51,143 --> 00:00:53,012
I came to visit my mother on Jeju Island...

23
00:00:53,013 --> 00:00:54,843
- after a long time. - Alkong.

24
00:00:54,943 --> 00:00:56,612
- Come here. - After our dog, Candy,

25
00:00:56,613 --> 00:00:58,582
passed away,

26
00:00:58,583 --> 00:01:01,123
the house felt very empty.

27
00:01:02,023 --> 00:01:05,223
We brought them home around last winter.

28
00:01:06,563 --> 00:01:10,533
(Alkong easily fell asleep in his mother's arms.)

29
00:01:11,263 --> 00:01:12,263
Alkong.

30
00:01:12,264 --> 00:01:14,132
- That's Alkong. - He's sticking out his tongue.

31
00:01:14,133 --> 00:01:15,133
(Pushing it out)

32
00:01:16,003 --> 00:01:20,143
(Dalkong stayed glued to Kian84.)

33
00:01:21,473 --> 00:01:23,812
- Dalkong. - This is Dalkong.

34
00:01:23,813 --> 00:01:25,273
- Alkong and Dalkong. - The timid one.

35
00:01:25,983 --> 00:01:29,952
Alkong is used to being around people.

36
00:01:29,953 --> 00:01:31,213
- He can get along with people. - Are you happy?

37
00:01:31,713 --> 00:01:33,722
He's a friendly dog.

38
00:01:33,723 --> 00:01:34,923
(He quickly adjusted to his new environment.)

39
00:01:35,623 --> 00:01:36,893
But Dalkong...

40
00:01:37,453 --> 00:01:40,862
- came from a puppy mill. Naturally, - I see.

41
00:01:40,863 --> 00:01:42,532
- That's not good. - he's very shy.

42
00:01:42,533 --> 00:01:43,562
- Right. - When we first brought him home,

43
00:01:43,563 --> 00:01:44,962
he didn't bark.

44
00:01:44,963 --> 00:01:47,402
- My goodness, he was scared. - He always felt intimidated.

45
00:01:47,403 --> 00:01:49,033
- He's shaking. It's okay. - Really?

46
00:01:50,273 --> 00:01:53,703
(After the fostering period ended last winter, they moved to Jeju.)

47
00:01:54,773 --> 00:01:56,072
They're living in a great place.

48
00:01:56,073 --> 00:01:58,912
Seriously. They can go to the beach every day. This is so nice.

49
00:01:58,913 --> 00:01:59,913
(They began their lives in Jeju known for clean water.)

50
00:02:00,513 --> 00:02:03,153
Alkong. Dalkong.

51
00:02:03,753 --> 00:02:04,913
They're so cute.

52
00:02:05,723 --> 00:02:07,682
Both humans and dogs need to be loved.

53
00:02:07,683 --> 00:02:08,922
- Of course. - That's right.

54
00:02:08,923 --> 00:02:11,393
I can tell your mother gave them a lot of love.

55
00:02:11,793 --> 00:02:15,563
Now that they're staying with my mother,

56
00:02:15,663 --> 00:02:17,833
they seemed more energetic than when we first met.

57
00:02:18,433 --> 00:02:21,232
They must feel confident now. They started to bark.

58
00:02:21,233 --> 00:02:22,972
- That's right. My goodness. - They're different now.

59
00:02:22,973 --> 00:02:24,232
Dalkong looks so energetic now.

60
00:02:24,233 --> 00:02:27,203
We have an old picture of him in a cage.

61
00:02:27,473 --> 00:02:29,002
- It will make you cry. Seriously. - Let's eat.

62
00:02:29,003 --> 00:02:30,113
- Let's go inside and eat. - Right.

63
00:02:30,413 --> 00:02:32,583
Let's go inside and eat.

64
00:02:32,983 --> 00:02:35,283
- Let's go inside and eat. - Go in.

65
00:02:35,853 --> 00:02:38,383
Let's go inside and eat.

66
00:02:38,713 --> 00:02:39,782
- Dalkong. - Jeju Island had only...

67
00:02:39,783 --> 00:02:41,623
- uphills. It was so exhausting. - Let's go in and eat. Hurry.

68
00:02:43,423 --> 00:02:45,023
- Hey. - Gosh, he feels comfortable there.

69
00:02:47,163 --> 00:02:49,663
(He plops down on the sofa as soon as he comes in.)

70
00:02:51,363 --> 00:02:55,973
She moved into this house 4 or 5 years ago.

71
00:02:58,573 --> 00:03:03,312
(Kian84's face is seen all over her house.)

72
00:03:03,313 --> 00:03:06,483
(A portrait of Kian84 personally drawn by his mother)

73
00:03:09,083 --> 00:03:10,382
Your mother... That's crazy.

74
00:03:10,383 --> 00:03:12,082
She started drawing for fun...

75
00:03:12,083 --> 00:03:13,753
- when she moved to Jeju Island. No. - But she never studied?

76
00:03:13,983 --> 00:03:15,483
She never took classes. It's her hobby.

77
00:03:16,053 --> 00:03:17,522
The artistic talent must run in the family.

78
00:03:17,523 --> 00:03:19,223
- Definitely. - You got the talent from her.

79
00:03:19,323 --> 00:03:20,593
It does run in the family.

80
00:03:22,093 --> 00:03:23,132
(Hyun Moo's painting is on display too.)

81
00:03:23,133 --> 00:03:24,933
- Wait. - That's Kian84.

82
00:03:24,993 --> 00:03:26,433
You're good at art, Hyun Moo.

83
00:03:27,203 --> 00:03:29,772
- Seriously. Your art has that vibe. - Why do you like my art so much?

84
00:03:29,773 --> 00:03:30,902
It looks great. I mean it.

85
00:03:30,903 --> 00:03:32,133
You didn't throw it out.

86
00:03:32,303 --> 00:03:34,403
I was wondering where it went. It turned out she had it.

87
00:03:35,173 --> 00:03:37,413
Are you serious? You didn't know where it had gone?

88
00:03:37,613 --> 00:03:39,343
- What was that supposed to be? - Come on.

89
00:03:39,713 --> 00:03:42,243
- What? - The letters on the painting.

90
00:03:42,683 --> 00:03:43,853
- Why did I write them? - It's completely different.

91
00:03:44,283 --> 00:03:45,612
- "Why?" - Did you forget?

92
00:03:45,613 --> 00:03:48,322
- Why did I write them? - "Fashion King." "Returning King."

93
00:03:48,323 --> 00:03:50,052
- The titles of my webtoons. - "Fashion King."

94
00:03:50,053 --> 00:03:52,023
How could you forget about your own art?

95
00:03:53,763 --> 00:03:56,063
Art is up to the interpretation of the recipient.

96
00:03:56,363 --> 00:03:58,632
- It's up to their interpretation. - That's right. I remember it now.

97
00:03:58,633 --> 00:03:59,703
Oh, right.

98
00:04:00,163 --> 00:04:01,762
(It's also full of cute ornaments.)

99
00:04:01,763 --> 00:04:03,332
I love your mom's house.

100
00:04:03,333 --> 00:04:05,333
I wonder why moms love owls.

101
00:04:05,503 --> 00:04:07,173
I know, right? My mom has them too.

102
00:04:09,343 --> 00:04:12,512
I sometimes use this room when I come over.

103
00:04:12,513 --> 00:04:14,812
But it's a guestroom, so it's a bit chilly inside.

104
00:04:14,813 --> 00:04:17,913
That's why it's not so comfortable to sleep in here.

105
00:04:19,183 --> 00:04:20,353
My house is much more comfortable.

106
00:04:21,953 --> 00:04:23,653
It's time for your meal now.

107
00:04:23,923 --> 00:04:25,693
Let's eat.

108
00:04:26,323 --> 00:04:27,922
You guys are going to eat.

109
00:04:27,923 --> 00:04:29,762
And your brother is going to eat too.

110
00:04:29,763 --> 00:04:30,763
"Brother."

111
00:04:30,863 --> 00:04:33,732
- Be quiet. - How many months has it been?

112
00:04:33,733 --> 00:04:34,763
How long?

113
00:04:35,133 --> 00:04:37,672
I brought them home on December 11.

114
00:04:37,673 --> 00:04:39,372
- They moved here in December? - Yes.

115
00:04:39,373 --> 00:04:40,503
It's already been four months?

116
00:04:40,743 --> 00:04:42,773
- It's been over four months. - Let me feed them.

117
00:04:43,373 --> 00:04:46,443
- Go inside the house for food. - They don't know what it is.

118
00:04:46,783 --> 00:04:48,012
- What? - To the house!

119
00:04:48,013 --> 00:04:49,082
(He confidently tries to feed them.)

120
00:04:49,083 --> 00:04:51,153
- Come on. To the house. - Alkong...

121
00:04:52,653 --> 00:04:54,123
Dalkong, to the house.

122
00:04:54,253 --> 00:04:57,553
They did a great grooming job. His legs are beautifully trimmed.

123
00:04:57,953 --> 00:04:59,363
That's what you're noticing now.

124
00:05:00,623 --> 00:05:02,732
- Dalkong, to the house. - Dalkong, to the house.

125
00:05:02,733 --> 00:05:03,862
Feed Alkong first.

126
00:05:03,863 --> 00:05:05,462
- Then Dalkong will figure it out. - Right.

127
00:05:05,463 --> 00:05:07,732
We're training them to go inside the kennel...

128
00:05:07,733 --> 00:05:09,373
- and eat their food there. - Dalkong, to the house.

129
00:05:09,903 --> 00:05:12,472
- Dalkong, to the house. - Go in if you want your food.

130
00:05:12,473 --> 00:05:14,273
- He can be scary. - To the house.

131
00:05:15,143 --> 00:05:17,142
I hadn't seen them in a while, so they didn't recognize me.

132
00:05:17,143 --> 00:05:19,043
- Really? - They were shy around me.

133
00:05:19,783 --> 00:05:20,943
Give it to him.

134
00:05:21,213 --> 00:05:23,112
He can be scary. Come here.

135
00:05:23,113 --> 00:05:25,012
Dalkong. Eat comfortably here.

136
00:05:25,013 --> 00:05:26,823
- Come here, Dalkong. - Why do they have to eat in there?

137
00:05:27,283 --> 00:05:29,393
- People train them to eat in there. - Kennel training is nice.

138
00:05:29,753 --> 00:05:31,052
- Why? - That way, they don't fight.

139
00:05:31,053 --> 00:05:32,723
- I see. So they won't fight. - Dogs can fight.

140
00:05:33,323 --> 00:05:34,723
- You go and wash up quickly. - Okay.

141
00:05:35,263 --> 00:05:36,493
I'll eat after I wash up.

142
00:05:37,393 --> 00:05:38,632
Hee Min, to the house.

143
00:05:38,633 --> 00:05:41,333
(Hee Min, to the house!)

144
00:05:41,633 --> 00:05:43,233
If you want food, go into the house.

145
00:05:43,433 --> 00:05:44,773
Hee Min, to the house.

146
00:05:45,833 --> 00:05:47,773
(Pulling out)

147
00:05:49,103 --> 00:05:51,873
I guess it's a rule that moms must have plates like that.

148
00:05:52,243 --> 00:05:53,743
- The national plate. - Right.

149
00:05:54,143 --> 00:05:55,213
The national plate.

150
00:05:57,953 --> 00:06:01,823
(As she thinks about her son who will enjoy her food,)

151
00:06:02,853 --> 00:06:03,853
(a hearty meal is coming together.)

152
00:06:03,854 --> 00:06:04,953
Food cooked by moms.

153
00:06:05,493 --> 00:06:06,563
That's the best.

154
00:06:07,993 --> 00:06:10,662
(Meanwhile, he's done with his shower.)

155
00:06:10,663 --> 00:06:11,663
(Feeling refreshed)

156
00:06:12,833 --> 00:06:15,433
- Hee Min, come on down. - Okay.

157
00:06:18,773 --> 00:06:19,773
(Grunting)

158
00:06:20,743 --> 00:06:22,072
See? You get so tired...

159
00:06:22,073 --> 00:06:23,413
- after a proper run. - Right.

160
00:06:23,513 --> 00:06:25,313
- I was so tired. - He's so fatigued.

161
00:06:26,113 --> 00:06:29,613
(He drags his tired body down for breakfast.)

162
00:06:30,353 --> 00:06:31,883
You want to come to me?

163
00:06:32,053 --> 00:06:34,423
Your brother is here. Let's give him his food.

164
00:06:35,053 --> 00:06:36,792
Your brother should eat now.

165
00:06:36,793 --> 00:06:38,553
Your brother should eat now. Hurry.

166
00:06:38,693 --> 00:06:39,763
Be quiet.

167
00:06:40,893 --> 00:06:42,493
Hey, he's smelling you.

168
00:06:42,833 --> 00:06:44,933
- Be quiet. - Give them...

169
00:06:45,493 --> 00:06:46,603
- What? - Give them...

170
00:06:46,803 --> 00:06:48,202
- Be quiet. - Give them your hand.

171
00:06:48,203 --> 00:06:49,473
- What? - Give them your hand.

172
00:06:49,603 --> 00:06:51,133
He's smelling you. There you go.

173
00:06:52,573 --> 00:06:54,373
- You don't remember him? - You must be a stranger to them.

174
00:06:54,503 --> 00:06:55,903
- They treated me like a stranger. - Right?

175
00:06:57,843 --> 00:06:59,012
Gosh, they're so loud.

176
00:06:59,013 --> 00:07:00,312
(The brothers need to get used to being around each other.)

177
00:07:00,313 --> 00:07:01,413
Come here.

178
00:07:01,913 --> 00:07:04,182
I was really craving some breakfast after a proper run.

179
00:07:04,183 --> 00:07:05,552
This is sea urchin roe.

180
00:07:05,553 --> 00:07:07,522
Wrap up some rice with the dried laver.

181
00:07:07,523 --> 00:07:09,622
- This is Jeju Island. - Jeju is in a different league.

182
00:07:09,623 --> 00:07:11,693
- This is a red tilefish. Eat it up. - Okay.

183
00:07:12,023 --> 00:07:13,922
The red tilefish was great with rice.

184
00:07:13,923 --> 00:07:14,993
This is Korean beef.

185
00:07:15,923 --> 00:07:17,233
She cooked Korean beef too.

186
00:07:18,333 --> 00:07:19,462
You're not going to eat?

187
00:07:19,463 --> 00:07:20,702
- Gosh, that looks so good. - I don't have an appetite.

188
00:07:20,703 --> 00:07:21,703
Oh, my. It looks great.

189
00:07:21,704 --> 00:07:23,103
(An epic spread of food)

190
00:07:23,533 --> 00:07:25,002
- Try the sea urchin roe first. - Okay.

191
00:07:25,003 --> 00:07:26,342
- Have the beef too. - The sea urchin roe?

192
00:07:26,343 --> 00:07:27,602
What? Try them together.

193
00:07:27,603 --> 00:07:29,303
My gosh. Look at that beautiful color.

194
00:07:30,073 --> 00:07:33,913
- When I brought them from Seoul... - Yes.

195
00:07:34,313 --> 00:07:35,413
Gosh, I'm getting hungry.

196
00:07:35,643 --> 00:07:38,553
- It was really good. - This is good.

197
00:07:38,883 --> 00:07:40,552
- Jeju is famous for red tilefish. - Jeju Island...

198
00:07:40,553 --> 00:07:41,583
It tastes so good.

199
00:07:41,823 --> 00:07:43,092
She deboned the fish for him.

200
00:07:43,093 --> 00:07:46,892
By the way, he's gotten more comfortable around people.

201
00:07:46,893 --> 00:07:48,322
Oh, definitely.

202
00:07:48,323 --> 00:07:51,563
Dalkong has improved so much. Isn't that right?

203
00:07:51,763 --> 00:07:52,862
I was worried at first.

204
00:07:52,863 --> 00:07:55,102
But he loves his teacher. He's a guy too.

205
00:07:55,103 --> 00:07:56,472
That's good, Mom. I'll do it myself.

206
00:07:56,473 --> 00:07:57,732
- He's a guy too. - That's good, Mom.

207
00:07:57,733 --> 00:07:58,773
I'll do it myself.

208
00:07:59,743 --> 00:08:01,203
No. Have some of this too.

209
00:08:01,743 --> 00:08:03,143
You keep telling me to eat.

210
00:08:03,513 --> 00:08:05,073
But still. You must eat more.

211
00:08:05,713 --> 00:08:07,083
They remind me of my mom and me.

212
00:08:07,613 --> 00:08:09,453
That's how it is for moms and sons.

213
00:08:09,653 --> 00:08:11,852
I was already eating, but she kept giving me more food.

214
00:08:11,853 --> 00:08:14,123
When you're trying to chew down the fish, she gives you tangerines.

215
00:08:14,253 --> 00:08:15,353
Right.

216
00:08:16,993 --> 00:08:19,052
(He takes a big bite, feeling self-conscious around her.)

217
00:08:19,053 --> 00:08:20,122
They're not barking at each other.

218
00:08:20,123 --> 00:08:22,833
- Right. They play with each other. - They're having fun together.

219
00:08:23,893 --> 00:08:25,262
Hyun Moo also adopted a dog.

220
00:08:25,263 --> 00:08:27,833
Right. Just yesterday, I texted the director of the shelter...

221
00:08:28,103 --> 00:08:30,273
where we brought Alkong and Dalkong.

222
00:08:30,573 --> 00:08:34,103
The director told me that Hyun Moo's mom adopted one too.

223
00:08:34,773 --> 00:08:38,343
Well, my parents feel lonely.

224
00:08:38,713 --> 00:08:41,043
Going to the hospital is the only thing they do.

225
00:08:41,513 --> 00:08:43,482
The chances of my getting married and giving them a grandchild...

226
00:08:43,483 --> 00:08:44,653
are pretty slim.

227
00:08:45,113 --> 00:08:48,283
So I thought giving them a new family member was better.

228
00:08:49,023 --> 00:08:51,722
I wanted to adopt one from a shelter and had been looking for one.

229
00:08:51,723 --> 00:08:53,893
That's when Kian84 and his mother adopted a dog.

230
00:08:54,123 --> 00:08:56,493
I asked him to introduce me to the director.

231
00:08:57,163 --> 00:09:00,302
We adopted a dog three days ago from the same shelter.

232
00:09:00,303 --> 00:09:01,303
- Really? - That was pretty recent.

233
00:09:01,303 --> 00:09:02,303
My parents are with the dog.

234
00:09:03,173 --> 00:09:05,003
- You have a new family member. - That's right.

235
00:09:05,433 --> 00:09:06,603
They're so happy now.

236
00:09:06,803 --> 00:09:08,743
We kept thinking about it. And we decided on this one.

237
00:09:09,203 --> 00:09:11,043
Adopting a dog from a shelter and running.

238
00:09:11,543 --> 00:09:13,583
Strangely, they keep doing the same thing.

239
00:09:14,613 --> 00:09:17,253
(A mysterious theory of the parallel universe)

240
00:09:17,483 --> 00:09:18,913
Ito Junji remains now.

241
00:09:20,523 --> 00:09:22,753
(Oh, no. Don't take him away from me.)

242
00:09:23,393 --> 00:09:25,453
I'm so busy in the morning because of them.

243
00:09:26,663 --> 00:09:30,762
They go to kindergarten three days a week.

244
00:09:30,763 --> 00:09:33,003
- Three days a week. - They go to kindergarten too?

245
00:09:33,663 --> 00:09:34,933
Dogs go to kindergarten?

246
00:09:35,063 --> 00:09:36,333
Apparently, they do.

247
00:09:37,173 --> 00:09:39,132
They go to kindergarten three days a week.

248
00:09:39,133 --> 00:09:41,473
- I drop them off and pick them up. - Seriously.

249
00:09:41,973 --> 00:09:43,513
(Grabbing)

250
00:09:43,773 --> 00:09:45,243
Gosh, they have a backpack.

251
00:09:45,573 --> 00:09:46,712
So cute.

252
00:09:46,713 --> 00:09:47,713
I need to pack their food.

253
00:09:47,843 --> 00:09:49,913
What kind of world are we living in now?

254
00:09:51,853 --> 00:09:54,083
(Confused)

255
00:09:54,483 --> 00:09:56,322
I need to pack their lunch.

256
00:09:56,323 --> 00:09:58,323
My gosh. She's packing their lunch.

257
00:09:58,623 --> 00:10:01,223
- I wonder where it had gone wrong. - No.

258
00:10:01,363 --> 00:10:04,263
You can't think that sending them there is wrong.

259
00:10:04,733 --> 00:10:07,203
They can go there and get trained.

260
00:10:07,663 --> 00:10:10,833
That way, they can enjoy their lives too.

261
00:10:11,003 --> 00:10:12,202
That's common these days.

262
00:10:12,203 --> 00:10:13,203
(He's on the same page.)

263
00:10:13,503 --> 00:10:15,103
- Okay. - Peekaboo.

264
00:10:15,503 --> 00:10:16,642
Let me pack your lunch.

265
00:10:16,643 --> 00:10:18,042
It's time for kindergarten.

266
00:10:18,043 --> 00:10:19,043
(Leaving her indifferent son behind...)

267
00:10:20,683 --> 00:10:22,312
She's packing their food.

268
00:10:22,313 --> 00:10:24,983
(She's packing their favorite dog food for lunch.)

269
00:10:26,583 --> 00:10:28,622
Dalkong. Alkong. Let's go to kindergarten.

270
00:10:28,623 --> 00:10:29,992
- Look at his backpack. - So cute.

271
00:10:29,993 --> 00:10:32,423
My goodness. He's gotten much more cheerful now.

272
00:10:33,423 --> 00:10:34,522
He won't even look at them.

273
00:10:34,523 --> 00:10:35,762
(I should just eat my food.)

274
00:10:35,763 --> 00:10:36,993
Let's go to kindergarten.

275
00:10:39,533 --> 00:10:41,302
(Reaching up)

276
00:10:41,303 --> 00:10:42,562
Gosh, he's such a cutie.

277
00:10:42,563 --> 00:10:44,132
They brighten up my mother's morning a lot.

278
00:10:44,133 --> 00:10:45,202
- She now... - Right.

279
00:10:45,203 --> 00:10:46,402
- That's right. - has a routine.

280
00:10:46,403 --> 00:10:49,142
- You're excited, Alkong. - You have a lovely mother.

281
00:10:49,143 --> 00:10:50,213
I see.

282
00:10:50,973 --> 00:10:52,772
- Do you want more rice? - No.

283
00:10:52,773 --> 00:10:54,643
- Yes? Why don't you... - I just want the tofu.

284
00:10:55,013 --> 00:10:56,112
You should eat a lot.

285
00:10:56,113 --> 00:10:58,013
(He goes after doenjang jjigae instead of getting more rice.)

286
00:10:59,083 --> 00:11:01,322
(Slurping)

287
00:11:01,323 --> 00:11:02,323
This is good.

288
00:11:02,683 --> 00:11:05,023
It's nice to see you enjoying the food.

289
00:11:05,193 --> 00:11:06,363
Moms love that the most.

290
00:11:06,793 --> 00:11:08,823
- Let me give you rice. It's salty. - No.

291
00:11:10,263 --> 00:11:12,132
(He keeps on slurping the stew without any rice.)

292
00:11:12,133 --> 00:11:14,363
Oh, my. You barely touched that expensive sea urchin roe.

293
00:11:14,463 --> 00:11:15,832
- Right. - Gosh, he didn't finish that?

294
00:11:15,833 --> 00:11:17,703
Why is your doenjang jjigae so good?

295
00:11:17,803 --> 00:11:18,803
- What? - Gosh.

296
00:11:19,403 --> 00:11:21,443
- Well, I'm... - This is your best doenjang jjigae.

297
00:11:21,573 --> 00:11:22,773
- It's the best doenjang jjigae? - Yes.

298
00:11:23,243 --> 00:11:25,442
- This is just crazy. - That's so sweet.

299
00:11:25,443 --> 00:11:26,513
- Really? - Yes.

300
00:11:26,643 --> 00:11:27,813
Did he really say that?

301
00:11:28,313 --> 00:11:30,253
Forget about ramyeon or gukbap.

302
00:11:31,213 --> 00:11:32,613
I don't need anything. This is the best.

303
00:11:34,083 --> 00:11:36,022
We both like food grannies like.

304
00:11:36,023 --> 00:11:37,553
No. Your doenjang jjigae is amazing.

305
00:11:37,693 --> 00:11:38,722
So you like it.

306
00:11:38,723 --> 00:11:40,793
- Thank you for the food. - Okay.

307
00:11:41,723 --> 00:11:46,033
Oh, no. I must hurry and drop them off at kindergarten.

308
00:11:46,093 --> 00:11:48,202
- Let's go to kindergarten, Dalkong. - They're late.

309
00:11:48,203 --> 00:11:49,733
It's time for kindergarten, Dalkong.

310
00:11:50,003 --> 00:11:51,332
When I mention kindergarten,

311
00:11:51,333 --> 00:11:52,702
- he comes for his leash. - He likes going there.

312
00:11:52,703 --> 00:11:55,573
- He loves going there. - Let's go to kindergarten, Dalkong.

313
00:11:55,773 --> 00:11:57,712
You're excited to see your brother.

314
00:11:57,713 --> 00:11:59,742
(They quickly get ready to go to kindergarten.)

315
00:11:59,743 --> 00:12:01,812
My goodness. They're so young.

316
00:12:01,813 --> 00:12:03,353
Take their backpacks.

317
00:12:03,553 --> 00:12:04,753
Take their backpacks.

318
00:12:05,713 --> 00:12:07,453
Put them on your shoulder.

319
00:12:07,553 --> 00:12:09,482
(Looking sour)

320
00:12:09,483 --> 00:12:11,653
- He doesn't like that. - Let's go.

321
00:12:13,263 --> 00:12:14,892
When dog kindergartens sprang up,

322
00:12:14,893 --> 00:12:16,493
it made me wonder...

323
00:12:17,593 --> 00:12:20,933
we were living in a transition period.

324
00:12:21,703 --> 00:12:23,833
- That's deep. - That crossed my mind.

325
00:12:23,903 --> 00:12:26,203
But on one hand,

326
00:12:26,403 --> 00:12:29,503
there must be a demand...

327
00:12:29,713 --> 00:12:32,513
for a service like this to exist.

328
00:12:33,613 --> 00:12:35,513
(A sharp inhale)

329
00:12:35,713 --> 00:12:37,283
It's quite puzzling.

330
00:12:38,383 --> 00:12:39,383
It is.

331
00:12:39,823 --> 00:12:41,252
I understand what you mean.

332
00:12:41,253 --> 00:12:43,493
When I first heard about dog kindergartens,

333
00:12:43,923 --> 00:12:46,862
it made me wonder for whose benefit they existed.

334
00:12:46,863 --> 00:12:48,462
- That was my first question. - You were objective.

335
00:12:48,463 --> 00:12:51,793
But they offered necessary training services.

336
00:12:52,163 --> 00:12:53,603
They can develop social skills. It's great.

337
00:12:53,803 --> 00:12:55,502
We can only spend limited time to play with them.

338
00:12:55,503 --> 00:12:56,602
- We feel bad. - That's right.

339
00:12:56,603 --> 00:12:57,632
- They can also go for walks. - We're busy.

340
00:12:57,633 --> 00:12:58,633
- Right. - They can go for walks...

341
00:12:58,634 --> 00:13:00,402
- and develop social skills. - That's right.

342
00:13:00,403 --> 00:13:02,773
We send them to kindergarten, so they can play with their friends.

343
00:13:02,873 --> 00:13:03,873
Dogs also get depressed...

344
00:13:03,874 --> 00:13:04,943
- when they only stay home. - Exactly.

345
00:13:05,713 --> 00:13:07,743
Let's go to kindergarten.

346
00:13:08,013 --> 00:13:09,413
- They leave quite early. - Let's go to kindergarten.

347
00:13:09,753 --> 00:13:11,653
- I'm curious what they do there. - Right here.

348
00:13:12,513 --> 00:13:13,682
- Let's go. - All right.

349
00:13:13,683 --> 00:13:15,653
Let's go and turn you into the smartest dog.

350
00:13:17,993 --> 00:13:20,323
- Go to your brother. - Come here.

351
00:13:21,493 --> 00:13:23,393
- Let's go. - Gosh, they're so cute.

352
00:13:23,493 --> 00:13:24,792
Let's go to kindergarten.

353
00:13:24,793 --> 00:13:27,562
- This is sort of like "SKY Castle." - Right.

354
00:13:27,563 --> 00:13:28,803
(They head out to the dog kindergarten.)

355
00:13:29,263 --> 00:13:30,503
Actually,

356
00:13:31,103 --> 00:13:35,042
I talked to the director of their shelter yesterday.

357
00:13:35,043 --> 00:13:36,713
- We text from time to time. - Okay.

358
00:13:36,873 --> 00:13:39,642
I heard all the dogs we met there got adopted.

359
00:13:39,643 --> 00:13:41,982
- Right. They were all adopted. - That's great news.

360
00:13:41,983 --> 00:13:43,452
- They all got adopted. - You helped them get adopted.

361
00:13:43,453 --> 00:13:44,783
- That's great news. - Right.

362
00:13:45,153 --> 00:13:46,782
- You took pictures of them. - Right.

363
00:13:46,783 --> 00:13:48,022
- Seriously. - They haven't found a home yet.

364
00:13:48,023 --> 00:13:49,183
- I just can't stop looking at them. - Oh, no.

365
00:13:49,653 --> 00:13:51,622
(He couldn't stop thinking about...)

366
00:13:51,623 --> 00:13:52,623
(the dogs who didn't get adopted.)

367
00:13:52,624 --> 00:13:54,063
- That's so important. - They got adopted, though.

368
00:13:54,723 --> 00:13:55,822
You're amazing.

369
00:13:55,823 --> 00:13:57,892
(They all found a new home and got adopted.)

370
00:13:57,893 --> 00:13:58,932
- They all went to nice families. - Yes.

371
00:13:58,933 --> 00:14:00,402
- What a relief. - I kept thinking about them.

372
00:14:00,403 --> 00:14:02,662
- So we were just chatting. - Okay.

373
00:14:02,663 --> 00:14:06,502
I told her that Dalkong became the president.

374
00:14:06,503 --> 00:14:08,002
(I told her that Dalkong became the president.)

375
00:14:08,003 --> 00:14:09,843
- What? - There's a president too?

376
00:14:10,373 --> 00:14:11,543
- I... - The president?

377
00:14:12,513 --> 00:14:14,743
- How do they pick the president? - You're not happy with it.

378
00:14:15,583 --> 00:14:18,282
- My gosh, the president? - They held an election for it.

379
00:14:18,283 --> 00:14:20,423
- Really? - They did.

380
00:14:20,723 --> 00:14:23,652
The number of likes on social media counted as votes.

381
00:14:23,653 --> 00:14:25,323
- The dog owners also voted. - I see.

382
00:14:25,553 --> 00:14:27,092
The dogs somehow voted too.

383
00:14:27,093 --> 00:14:28,692
- That's a democratic way to do it. - Right.

384
00:14:28,693 --> 00:14:30,332
- I guess they go to the candidate. - Like that?

385
00:14:30,333 --> 00:14:31,593
I guess that's how they vote.

386
00:14:32,193 --> 00:14:33,363
(Laughing)

387
00:14:33,463 --> 00:14:35,603
They also have sports days and so on.

388
00:14:35,733 --> 00:14:36,872
- Sports days? - They do.

389
00:14:36,873 --> 00:14:38,002
- They have sports days. - They play dodgeball together.

390
00:14:38,003 --> 00:14:39,943
They have events for Puppy Day,

391
00:14:40,273 --> 00:14:41,502
- That's cute. - Parents' Day,

392
00:14:41,503 --> 00:14:42,772
- and so on. - Oh, that's so cute.

393
00:14:42,773 --> 00:14:44,912
They're doing things her grandchildren should do...

394
00:14:44,913 --> 00:14:46,342
because I failed to give her one.

395
00:14:46,343 --> 00:14:47,513
We're in the same boat.

396
00:14:47,743 --> 00:14:49,313
I couldn't give them grandchildren.

397
00:14:49,643 --> 00:14:50,713
I adopted a dog for them.

398
00:14:51,653 --> 00:14:53,322
I should keep an open mind today.

399
00:14:53,323 --> 00:14:55,422
Yes. You should be open to many possibilities.

400
00:14:55,423 --> 00:14:56,523
Okay. Yes, sir.

401
00:14:56,893 --> 00:14:57,992
Today's episode is fun.

402
00:14:57,993 --> 00:14:59,023
(Intrigued)

403
00:15:00,023 --> 00:15:02,593
(The brother hears the news about Dalkong's presidency.)

404
00:15:02,893 --> 00:15:04,363
- I... - The president?

405
00:15:04,893 --> 00:15:08,962
This time around, there were six candidates.

406
00:15:08,963 --> 00:15:11,233
You mean, six dogs. They're not real people.

407
00:15:11,903 --> 00:15:13,743
They're like my own children.

408
00:15:13,803 --> 00:15:15,842
I don't see them as mere dogs.

409
00:15:15,843 --> 00:15:18,142
- I'm proud of Dalkong. - My gosh, it was like a festival.

410
00:15:18,143 --> 00:15:19,543
So...

411
00:15:19,843 --> 00:15:21,742
- Should I be happy about this? - What?

412
00:15:21,743 --> 00:15:23,083
Does he have any power as the president?

413
00:15:23,713 --> 00:15:25,282
- Who knows? - Since he's the president,

414
00:15:25,283 --> 00:15:26,652
ask the kindergarten to give us a discount.

415
00:15:26,653 --> 00:15:27,722
What?

416
00:15:27,723 --> 00:15:29,252
Ask them for a discount or something.

417
00:15:29,253 --> 00:15:32,123
- Anyway, I thought it was so funny. - Okay.

418
00:15:32,323 --> 00:15:34,092
I decided to give out rice cakes...

419
00:15:34,093 --> 00:15:36,192
- She looks so happy. - to celebrate his presidency.

420
00:15:36,193 --> 00:15:37,792
- She'll give out rice cakes? - I really should.

421
00:15:37,793 --> 00:15:39,432
This is a great occasion for rice cakes.

422
00:15:39,433 --> 00:15:40,532
I'm not sure about this.

423
00:15:40,533 --> 00:15:43,802
But the problem is Dalkong doesn't know he's the president.

424
00:15:43,803 --> 00:15:44,902
- He knows. - Exactly.

425
00:15:44,903 --> 00:15:46,103
He'd never know.

426
00:15:46,303 --> 00:15:48,573
He must know. I'm sure he's proud of himself.

427
00:15:48,673 --> 00:15:50,643
So my friends and I texted a lot...

428
00:15:50,913 --> 00:15:52,972
about the election.

429
00:15:52,973 --> 00:15:55,913
They wrote things like, "Dalkong for President."

430
00:15:56,183 --> 00:15:58,253
We had so much fun with the election.

431
00:15:58,653 --> 00:16:00,182
(Confused)

432
00:16:00,183 --> 00:16:01,583
I bet there are so many things he wants to say.

433
00:16:01,683 --> 00:16:04,453
A while ago, she started...

434
00:16:04,893 --> 00:16:06,193
texting me.

435
00:16:06,623 --> 00:16:08,963
She asked me to write a comment and vote on some platform.

436
00:16:09,693 --> 00:16:10,763
I asked her about it.

437
00:16:11,533 --> 00:16:14,533
She told me that Dalkong was running for president.

438
00:16:15,103 --> 00:16:16,803
I asked her not to do stuff like that.

439
00:16:17,233 --> 00:16:18,303
Why not?

440
00:16:18,533 --> 00:16:20,702
There were campaign pledges. They were even more annoying.

441
00:16:20,703 --> 00:16:22,243
- What were the campaign pledges? - The campaign pledges were...

442
00:16:23,573 --> 00:16:26,173
"I will share my snacks..."

443
00:16:26,983 --> 00:16:28,283
"with my friends."

444
00:16:29,113 --> 00:16:30,253
That's so cute!

445
00:16:31,153 --> 00:16:32,183
Dalkong.

446
00:16:32,483 --> 00:16:33,522
- That's so adorable. - "I'll make..."

447
00:16:33,523 --> 00:16:35,422
- Oh, my goodness. - "a happy environment for dogs."

448
00:16:35,423 --> 00:16:36,553
That's so cute.

449
00:16:36,653 --> 00:16:38,722
Seriously. I don't even know...

450
00:16:38,723 --> 00:16:40,423
how I'm supposed to feel about that.

451
00:16:40,663 --> 00:16:44,133
I saw these crazy promises on the campaign poster.

452
00:16:45,363 --> 00:16:46,562
- He's not exactly wrong. - It's...

453
00:16:46,563 --> 00:16:48,463
- It's true. They do sound crazy. - Right. He's not wrong.

454
00:16:48,503 --> 00:16:50,502
- Right. - But my mother...

455
00:16:50,503 --> 00:16:52,702
- and other dog owners... - Right.

456
00:16:52,703 --> 00:16:54,843
were having so much fun with it.

457
00:16:55,203 --> 00:16:56,742
- So I thought it was meaningful. - Right.

458
00:16:56,743 --> 00:16:57,812
That's what I thought.

459
00:16:57,813 --> 00:16:59,312
- And dogs look happy too. - Right.

460
00:16:59,313 --> 00:17:00,882
- That's true too. - Right.

461
00:17:00,883 --> 00:17:02,382
I'm glad that...

462
00:17:02,383 --> 00:17:04,213
- they make my mom happy. - Right.

463
00:17:04,753 --> 00:17:05,853
Seriously.

464
00:17:08,453 --> 00:17:11,123
By the way, I signed Dalkong up...

465
00:17:11,693 --> 00:17:14,393
for the election for a reason.

466
00:17:14,893 --> 00:17:16,362
- Alkong... - For his social skills?

467
00:17:16,363 --> 00:17:17,462
doesn't need me.

468
00:17:17,463 --> 00:17:19,563
- Right. - But my heart breaks for Dalkong.

469
00:17:19,663 --> 00:17:22,402
He's still shy and nervous around people.

470
00:17:22,403 --> 00:17:24,772
I signed Dalkong up for the election,

471
00:17:24,773 --> 00:17:26,972
so his teacher would pay more attention to him.

472
00:17:26,973 --> 00:17:29,703
That was the reason behind my decision.

473
00:17:29,973 --> 00:17:31,872
And his teacher told me that...

474
00:17:31,873 --> 00:17:33,882
Dalkong improved a lot over these few months.

475
00:17:33,883 --> 00:17:35,083
- I can see his improvement too. - Okay.

476
00:17:35,283 --> 00:17:37,313
Do you know what he does when I drop him off?

477
00:17:37,453 --> 00:17:39,252
He'd look at me with these sad eyes.

478
00:17:39,253 --> 00:17:41,123
- Then he'd follow his teacher. Yes. - He'd still go?

479
00:17:42,023 --> 00:17:44,052
He needs to improve his social skills.

480
00:17:44,053 --> 00:17:45,922
I went to kindergarten too. How come I don't have social skills?

481
00:17:45,923 --> 00:17:48,193
(I went to kindergarten too. How come I don't have social skills?)

482
00:17:48,523 --> 00:17:49,823
Gosh, it's only kindergarten.

483
00:17:50,133 --> 00:17:52,962
Gosh, people don't turn out the same even if they go to kindergarten.

484
00:17:52,963 --> 00:17:55,203
- Right. - Everyone is different.

485
00:17:55,303 --> 00:17:56,902
Look at Alkong and Dalkong.

486
00:17:56,903 --> 00:17:59,172
- They're completely different. - Right.

487
00:17:59,173 --> 00:18:01,572
It's the same with people. It depends on the personality.

488
00:18:01,573 --> 00:18:04,542
- Right. - You know? Everyone knows that.

489
00:18:04,543 --> 00:18:07,112
But it's more touching that the introverted one, Dalkong,

490
00:18:07,113 --> 00:18:08,613
became the president.

491
00:18:10,683 --> 00:18:12,753
I heard there were special districts for dog schools these days.

492
00:18:13,423 --> 00:18:16,082
Gosh, some people send their dogs to English kindergarten.

493
00:18:16,083 --> 00:18:18,292
- What? - English kindergarten for dogs?

494
00:18:18,293 --> 00:18:19,693
How could they go to English kindergarten?

495
00:18:20,123 --> 00:18:21,562
(Shocked)

496
00:18:21,563 --> 00:18:22,563
That's what I heard.

497
00:18:23,293 --> 00:18:25,192
(The news of the new world makes him laugh in disbelief.)

498
00:18:25,193 --> 00:18:26,433
That's crazy.

499
00:18:26,733 --> 00:18:28,133
Oh, I think I know what it is.

500
00:18:28,263 --> 00:18:30,102
Some people train their dogs in English like, "Sit."

501
00:18:30,103 --> 00:18:31,302
- "Sit." - "Sit."

502
00:18:31,303 --> 00:18:32,472
- "Wait." - "Stand."

503
00:18:32,473 --> 00:18:34,202
- Some dogs get adopted abroad. - No. That's...

504
00:18:34,203 --> 00:18:35,902
- Right. - That makes sense then.

505
00:18:35,903 --> 00:18:39,642
The thing is, I keep finding both sides understandable.

506
00:18:39,643 --> 00:18:40,883
So I'm caught in the middle.

507
00:18:41,283 --> 00:18:42,943
I never went to kindergarten.

508
00:18:45,013 --> 00:18:46,723
My nose is tickling.

509
00:18:48,923 --> 00:18:49,923
We're here.

510
00:18:50,853 --> 00:18:52,693
Become amazing dogs.

511
00:18:54,623 --> 00:18:57,033
My gosh, it's up.

512
00:18:57,593 --> 00:18:59,963
Goodness, Dalkong is up there.

513
00:19:00,833 --> 00:19:02,862
Goodness, Dalkong is up there.

514
00:19:02,863 --> 00:19:03,902
Oh, my.

515
00:19:03,903 --> 00:19:05,102
(His picture is on the banner at the entrance.)

516
00:19:05,103 --> 00:19:06,372
- My gosh. - Okay.

517
00:19:06,373 --> 00:19:07,402
- Congratulations. - Gosh.

518
00:19:07,403 --> 00:19:08,642
(The president of Class Good Manners is here.)

519
00:19:08,643 --> 00:19:09,643
You've won.

520
00:19:10,413 --> 00:19:12,142
I see. Each class has a president.

521
00:19:12,143 --> 00:19:13,682
- The photos of presidents are up. - "The president."

522
00:19:13,683 --> 00:19:14,913
- "The vice-president." - "The vice-president."

523
00:19:15,113 --> 00:19:16,282
Chui is the vice-president.

524
00:19:16,283 --> 00:19:17,853
This is just like Daechi-dong.

525
00:19:21,783 --> 00:19:24,493
Alkong. Dalkong.

526
00:19:25,053 --> 00:19:26,392
- Dalkong. - That's good. Open the door.

527
00:19:26,393 --> 00:19:27,693
Their backpacks. Hold on.

528
00:19:28,693 --> 00:19:30,462
- He's annoyed. - Gosh, Dalkong.

529
00:19:30,463 --> 00:19:32,193
(He spots something and walks over.)

530
00:19:32,663 --> 00:19:33,703
This is crazy.

531
00:19:34,903 --> 00:19:35,932
(Campaign posters are hanging at the gate.)

532
00:19:35,933 --> 00:19:38,532
- What? "Park Boknam?" - Participation is all that matters.

533
00:19:38,533 --> 00:19:39,832
"He won the battle with Park Boknam."

534
00:19:39,833 --> 00:19:41,042
(Among the pocket-sized candidates...)

535
00:19:41,043 --> 00:19:43,473
- They all had campaign pledges. - Of course.

536
00:19:44,013 --> 00:19:45,072
(The gallant Dalkong threw his hat into the ring.)

537
00:19:45,073 --> 00:19:46,273
So cute.

538
00:19:46,543 --> 00:19:48,213
Dalkong looks so gallant.

539
00:19:48,543 --> 00:19:50,783
(And...)

540
00:19:51,853 --> 00:19:53,182
Oh, my. Look at Dalkong.

541
00:19:53,183 --> 00:19:56,123
(he was elected president.)

542
00:19:56,223 --> 00:19:57,492
That's something else.

543
00:19:57,493 --> 00:19:58,622
(The congratulatory banners are everywhere.)

544
00:19:58,623 --> 00:20:00,293
Seriously. Unbelievable.

545
00:20:01,493 --> 00:20:02,822
(He brought glory to his family.)

546
00:20:02,823 --> 00:20:03,933
Look at his eyes.

547
00:20:04,193 --> 00:20:05,263
Look at his charisma.

548
00:20:05,693 --> 00:20:07,003
"I'll take care of my class."

549
00:20:07,833 --> 00:20:10,272
- Look at him. He looks so reliable. - Over here.

550
00:20:10,273 --> 00:20:11,533
(Adoring)

551
00:20:11,903 --> 00:20:14,502
- Which big dog ended up winning? - Among...

552
00:20:14,503 --> 00:20:16,372
This one. Sunny became the president.

553
00:20:16,373 --> 00:20:17,443
Sunny won?

554
00:20:17,943 --> 00:20:19,513
Sunny looks like the president.

555
00:20:19,613 --> 00:20:23,482
I heard that this one lost the election four years in a row.

556
00:20:23,483 --> 00:20:24,713
Hodu lost four years in a row?

557
00:20:24,913 --> 00:20:27,222
- This was his fourth election? - This time around...

558
00:20:27,223 --> 00:20:29,022
- He tried out for the 4th time? - Was it his 5th try?

559
00:20:29,023 --> 00:20:31,292
- Yes. It was his fourth try. - Oh, no. Hodu.

560
00:20:31,293 --> 00:20:32,653
- Hodu, change the campaign pledges. - Hodu.

561
00:20:33,923 --> 00:20:35,023
Poor Hodu.

562
00:20:35,963 --> 00:20:36,993
I wonder if he'll try again.

563
00:20:37,193 --> 00:20:38,632
Will Hodu run for the fifth time?

564
00:20:38,633 --> 00:20:40,602
What? Hodu became the president this time.

565
00:20:40,603 --> 00:20:41,603
Hodu became the president?

566
00:20:41,604 --> 00:20:44,033
(Hodu finally became the president.)

567
00:20:44,403 --> 00:20:45,833
- Hello. - Teacher.

568
00:20:46,033 --> 00:20:47,642
- Hello. - Hello.

569
00:20:47,643 --> 00:20:48,903
Alkong and Dalkong.

570
00:20:49,243 --> 00:20:50,442
- How cute. - Hello.

571
00:20:50,443 --> 00:20:51,542
- Please put Dalkong down. - Okay.

572
00:20:51,543 --> 00:20:52,843
Please put Alkong and Dalkong down.

573
00:20:53,883 --> 00:20:55,142
- My goodness. - They're excited.

574
00:20:55,143 --> 00:20:56,212
- Hee Min. - Yes?

575
00:20:56,213 --> 00:20:57,382
- She is the assistant director... - Okay.

576
00:20:57,383 --> 00:20:58,383
and he is the homeroom teacher.

577
00:20:58,384 --> 00:21:00,353
- The homeroom teacher? - So there is a homeroom teacher.

578
00:21:00,553 --> 00:21:02,452
They adore the dogs.

579
00:21:02,453 --> 00:21:03,623
(They adore Alkong and Dalkong.)

580
00:21:03,793 --> 00:21:05,452
- Thank you. - We'll go inside...

581
00:21:05,453 --> 00:21:07,062
- after walking around the yard. - Okay.

582
00:21:07,063 --> 00:21:08,323
- Let's go, Alkong and Dalkong. - Let's go.

583
00:21:08,593 --> 00:21:10,063
Alkong and Dalkong, let's go.

584
00:21:10,633 --> 00:21:13,363
(Staring)

585
00:21:13,533 --> 00:21:15,902
- Does being the president have... - Let's go.

586
00:21:15,903 --> 00:21:17,933
- any perks? - There should be perks.

587
00:21:18,073 --> 00:21:20,572
One perk is the letter of appointment.

588
00:21:20,573 --> 00:21:23,173
- The letter of appointment. - Also, as you can see,

589
00:21:23,273 --> 00:21:24,813
- the elected ones get celebrated. - Right.

590
00:21:25,043 --> 00:21:26,142
(I see.)

591
00:21:26,143 --> 00:21:28,842
I'll bring some rice cake next week.

592
00:21:28,843 --> 00:21:31,812
- Dalkong became the president. - So I should bring some rice cake.

593
00:21:31,813 --> 00:21:34,122
- Dalkong became the president. - The winner usually buys pizza.

594
00:21:34,123 --> 00:21:35,123
- That's right. - All right.

595
00:21:35,123 --> 00:21:36,123
(All right.)

596
00:21:36,124 --> 00:21:37,153
They dote on their mom.

597
00:21:37,323 --> 00:21:39,322
They dote on their mom. They only follow their mom.

598
00:21:39,323 --> 00:21:41,463
- Seriously. - Teacher.

599
00:21:42,023 --> 00:21:43,492
Let's make a round...

600
00:21:43,493 --> 00:21:45,193
before going into the classroom.

601
00:21:45,533 --> 00:21:46,563
All right.

602
00:21:47,233 --> 00:21:49,362
Dalkong dotes on his mom.

603
00:21:49,363 --> 00:21:52,232
Don't look at me when you run. Look ahead when you run.

604
00:21:52,233 --> 00:21:53,602
Don't look at me.

605
00:21:53,603 --> 00:21:55,873
- For midterms, - Okay.

606
00:21:56,073 --> 00:21:58,812
they practice how to walk alongside people.

607
00:21:58,813 --> 00:22:00,212
- He is walking well. - Is he walking well?

608
00:22:00,213 --> 00:22:01,883
- Yes. He is doing an amazing job. - Midterms.

609
00:22:02,143 --> 00:22:03,782
Soon, they'll take exams.

610
00:22:03,783 --> 00:22:04,982
- What exams? - Midterms are in May.

611
00:22:04,983 --> 00:22:06,712
They'll get tested on walking well by their parents' side.

612
00:22:06,713 --> 00:22:08,353
He is doing a good job now.

613
00:22:08,653 --> 00:22:11,053
Dalkong is doing a good job now.

614
00:22:11,123 --> 00:22:15,322
Is it like a three-legged race? Is it like a field day?

615
00:22:15,323 --> 00:22:18,092
We have midterms. We have two midterms a year.

616
00:22:18,093 --> 00:22:19,193
(Midterms, not a field day)

617
00:22:19,433 --> 00:22:21,102
We have two midterms a year.

618
00:22:21,103 --> 00:22:23,672
During midterms, they get tested on commands such as "Sit" and "Wait..."

619
00:22:23,673 --> 00:22:25,532
or their ability to walk alongside people.

620
00:22:25,533 --> 00:22:26,872
They can't cram for such exams.

621
00:22:26,873 --> 00:22:27,942
- Of course not. - No.

622
00:22:27,943 --> 00:22:29,002
- They need to do it regularly. - They need to practice daily.

623
00:22:29,003 --> 00:22:30,003
Yes. They need to do it regularly.

624
00:22:30,004 --> 00:22:32,813
We'll score their performances and give them grades.

625
00:22:33,343 --> 00:22:34,942
- Grades. - Yes.

626
00:22:34,943 --> 00:22:36,342
(We'll give them grades for the midterms.)

627
00:22:36,343 --> 00:22:37,383
I felt like...

628
00:22:38,183 --> 00:22:40,083
I was in a strange dream.

629
00:22:41,183 --> 00:22:43,583
I felt like those dogs were my children...

630
00:22:43,753 --> 00:22:47,022
and my mom's grandchildren.

631
00:22:47,023 --> 00:22:48,663
- It's like webcomics. - Right.

632
00:22:50,363 --> 00:22:51,463
This is driving me crazy.

633
00:22:51,793 --> 00:22:53,032
I hope they place first.

634
00:22:53,033 --> 00:22:55,503
If they place first, they'll receive an excellence award.

635
00:22:55,663 --> 00:22:57,033
Awards will pile up at home.

636
00:22:57,233 --> 00:22:59,903
(Feeling dizzy)

637
00:23:01,073 --> 00:23:02,903
- Please take good care of them. - Okay.

638
00:23:03,173 --> 00:23:04,742
- See you later, Mr. Kim. - You seem like the dad.

639
00:23:04,743 --> 00:23:05,872
- See you later. - Okay.

640
00:23:05,873 --> 00:23:07,212
Thank you.

641
00:23:07,213 --> 00:23:08,213
- See you later. - Okay.

642
00:23:08,214 --> 00:23:09,482
Visit them on Teachers' Day, okay?

643
00:23:09,483 --> 00:23:12,783
(Visit them on Teachers' Day, okay?)

644
00:23:13,153 --> 00:23:15,623
(After dropping the dogs off, they head somewhere.)

645
00:23:16,653 --> 00:23:18,023
The kindergarten is so funny.

646
00:23:18,623 --> 00:23:21,593
They have exams and midterms. That's so funny.

647
00:23:21,893 --> 00:23:24,962
In May, they'll also have a Children's Day event.

648
00:23:24,963 --> 00:23:26,793
Moms find that fun.

649
00:23:26,963 --> 00:23:28,362
- They know it's ridiculous. - Such things...

650
00:23:28,363 --> 00:23:30,102
- entertain her. - That's right. It's happiness.

651
00:23:30,103 --> 00:23:32,303
Will it rain? What's up with the weather?

652
00:23:32,773 --> 00:23:34,872
They said the sky would clear up in the afternoon.

653
00:23:34,873 --> 00:23:36,573
It won't rain.

654
00:23:38,843 --> 00:23:39,943
I think we're here.

655
00:23:41,413 --> 00:23:43,882
Is this the place? It's here. It isn't big, is it?

656
00:23:43,883 --> 00:23:45,052
- It's small. - Yes.

657
00:23:45,053 --> 00:23:46,613
- Let's park here, Mom. - Okay.

658
00:23:47,783 --> 00:23:48,953
Did she buy a plot of land?

659
00:23:49,283 --> 00:23:50,923
She didn't buy a plot of land.

660
00:23:52,193 --> 00:23:56,223
When the dogs moved from the mainland to the island,

661
00:23:56,423 --> 00:23:58,663
one person could board the plane with one dog.

662
00:23:58,963 --> 00:24:01,602
Since my mom couldn't take both of them,

663
00:24:01,603 --> 00:24:06,372
her friend traveled all the way to Seoul to help her...

664
00:24:06,373 --> 00:24:09,902
and boarded the plane with the other dog.

665
00:24:09,903 --> 00:24:11,072
(Her friend accompanied her to Seoul and brought Alkong and Dalkong.)

666
00:24:11,073 --> 00:24:13,173
- They brought the dogs that way. - I'm so thankful.

667
00:24:13,943 --> 00:24:17,153
My mom was indebted to her,

668
00:24:17,313 --> 00:24:19,553
so she asked me to help with some farm work.

669
00:24:19,813 --> 00:24:22,023
I went there because there was a lot of work.

670
00:24:22,453 --> 00:24:24,492
I don't know which variety it is.

671
00:24:24,493 --> 00:24:25,693
- I think they're here. - Yes.

672
00:24:26,153 --> 00:24:27,662
- Jeong Hye. - Hello.

673
00:24:27,663 --> 00:24:28,863
- Hello. - Hello.

674
00:24:29,363 --> 00:24:31,392
- Auntie who brought Alkong... - Hello.

675
00:24:31,393 --> 00:24:32,532
- and her husband. - Thank you.

676
00:24:32,533 --> 00:24:33,662
You can give me any kind of work.

677
00:24:33,663 --> 00:24:34,762
- That's right. - Will you be able to do it?

678
00:24:34,763 --> 00:24:37,303
- You look quite strong. - Give him a lot of work.

679
00:24:37,403 --> 00:24:39,372
Since you help me all the time,

680
00:24:39,373 --> 00:24:41,473
he can work for a day.

681
00:24:41,873 --> 00:24:43,402
(Nodding)

682
00:24:43,403 --> 00:24:44,942
The stone wall...

683
00:24:44,943 --> 00:24:46,672
- needs to be repaired. - I see the damage.

684
00:24:46,673 --> 00:24:49,383
We need to repair the stone wall and cut down the bamboo.

685
00:24:49,513 --> 00:24:50,752
I can use my muscles.

686
00:24:50,753 --> 00:24:51,912
- Make him use his muscles. - I like that.

687
00:24:51,913 --> 00:24:53,613
Make him work like a servant.

688
00:24:54,283 --> 00:24:55,752
(Servant DNA stirs at the thought of working.)

689
00:24:55,753 --> 00:24:57,193
Servant DNA.

690
00:24:57,523 --> 00:24:59,593
Hee Min is an incredible worker.

691
00:25:00,793 --> 00:25:01,893
Servant84.

692
00:25:02,293 --> 00:25:03,523
Servant84.

693
00:25:04,663 --> 00:25:07,132
Mom, go and have a cup of coffee.

694
00:25:07,133 --> 00:25:08,232
Okay.

695
00:25:08,233 --> 00:25:09,862
- I can't work because of my back. - How cool!

696
00:25:09,863 --> 00:25:10,872
Okay.

697
00:25:10,873 --> 00:25:12,302
- Jeong Hye! - Yes?

698
00:25:12,303 --> 00:25:15,342
Go ahead and work with Hee Min.

699
00:25:15,343 --> 00:25:16,673
I'll get going.

700
00:25:17,873 --> 00:25:19,272
Okay, President's Mom.

701
00:25:19,273 --> 00:25:22,883
(Okay, President's Mom.)

702
00:25:23,643 --> 00:25:24,813
He doesn't like that.

703
00:25:25,883 --> 00:25:28,623
(The president's mom takes a break.)

704
00:25:28,953 --> 00:25:31,392
(This servant gets left behind.)

705
00:25:31,393 --> 00:25:32,822
- This is the stone wall. - Was it very windy?

706
00:25:32,823 --> 00:25:34,162
How did it collapse?

707
00:25:34,163 --> 00:25:36,422
When the workers were cutting down the coastal hogfennel,

708
00:25:36,423 --> 00:25:38,392
- the stone wall collapsed. - It happened during work.

709
00:25:38,393 --> 00:25:40,333
- Yes, it happened during work. - It can withstand wind.

710
00:25:40,603 --> 00:25:42,033
- Place rock like this. - Okay.

711
00:25:42,433 --> 00:25:43,632
If you keep circling it,

712
00:25:43,633 --> 00:25:45,132
- it'll stop moving at a point. - Okay.

713
00:25:45,133 --> 00:25:46,533
- That's how you know it's in place. - I see.

714
00:25:46,773 --> 00:25:48,902
It's even now, right?

715
00:25:48,903 --> 00:25:49,973
- Okay. - Here.

716
00:25:51,273 --> 00:25:53,512
(They begin to build the stone wall.)

717
00:25:53,513 --> 00:25:55,582
My goodness. It's hard work.

718
00:25:55,583 --> 00:25:56,642
I know.

719
00:25:56,643 --> 00:25:57,682
(Heave-ho)

720
00:25:57,683 --> 00:25:58,952
You pile up rocks like that.

721
00:25:58,953 --> 00:26:01,583
Basalt rocks are perfect for piling up.

722
00:26:01,783 --> 00:26:03,382
- They're light. - Yes.

723
00:26:03,383 --> 00:26:04,453
They're bumpy too.

724
00:26:06,623 --> 00:26:08,392
What's fascinating is that a well-built wall doesn't collapse.

725
00:26:08,393 --> 00:26:09,722
- Really? - The rocks aren't glued.

726
00:26:09,723 --> 00:26:11,233
The rocks fit together like puzzle pieces.

727
00:26:11,533 --> 00:26:12,733
It's like "Tetris."

728
00:26:13,563 --> 00:26:15,832
(Oh, my!)

729
00:26:15,833 --> 00:26:18,373
- What is it? - Look at this worm.

730
00:26:18,673 --> 00:26:19,732
Thanks to the quality soil,

731
00:26:19,733 --> 00:26:20,803
- the worms get big. - Look at this worm.

732
00:26:21,373 --> 00:26:23,273
- How is it so big? - What?

733
00:26:23,503 --> 00:26:24,572
- Is that a snake? - It's so big.

734
00:26:24,573 --> 00:26:25,573
It's big.

735
00:26:26,143 --> 00:26:28,643
(Staring)

736
00:26:29,243 --> 00:26:30,512
Will you run to become the president?

737
00:26:30,513 --> 00:26:32,182
(By any chance, will you run to become the president?)

738
00:26:32,183 --> 00:26:33,583
Let's become the president.

739
00:26:33,923 --> 00:26:35,383
The worm should go to the kindergarten.

740
00:26:35,883 --> 00:26:36,953
The kindergarten.

741
00:26:38,223 --> 00:26:39,793
The worm has midterms next week.

742
00:26:41,623 --> 00:26:43,263
They'll get tested on digging into the soil fast.

743
00:26:43,893 --> 00:26:47,303
They have midterms next week, so that worm is practicing.

744
00:26:47,403 --> 00:26:48,463
Digging into the soil!

745
00:26:51,203 --> 00:26:52,203
Go back.

746
00:26:52,204 --> 00:26:53,572
(Go back and study for the exams.)

747
00:26:53,573 --> 00:26:54,573
Seriously.

748
00:26:54,574 --> 00:26:55,843
(Putting down)

749
00:26:56,803 --> 00:26:59,113
(Looking for rocks to pile up)

750
00:26:59,773 --> 00:27:01,583
- Oh, my! - Is there another one?

751
00:27:02,443 --> 00:27:03,513
It's a pill bug.

752
00:27:04,513 --> 00:27:05,813
A cute pill bug.

753
00:27:06,853 --> 00:27:09,483
Thanks to the quality soil, you can find all sorts of things.

754
00:27:10,953 --> 00:27:12,293
Do you go to the kindergarten too?

755
00:27:12,523 --> 00:27:15,023
Are you the president too?

756
00:27:15,523 --> 00:27:17,163
- They learn how to roll up fast. - How to roll up.

757
00:27:18,233 --> 00:27:20,763
(Go and study for the exams.)

758
00:27:21,933 --> 00:27:24,333
(After keeping a bug from studying, he gets back to work.)

759
00:27:25,473 --> 00:27:27,102
(Piling up rocks)

760
00:27:27,103 --> 00:27:28,773
(Piling up more rocks)

761
00:27:29,743 --> 00:27:31,513
(Lifting)

762
00:27:32,013 --> 00:27:34,512
(Servant84 can even lift up a boulder.)

763
00:27:34,513 --> 00:27:35,983
- It's a big one. - Be careful.

764
00:27:36,213 --> 00:27:37,253
Okay.

765
00:27:37,853 --> 00:27:39,953
(Heave-ho)

766
00:27:40,483 --> 00:27:42,283
- Moving rocks suits you. - Yes.

767
00:27:43,383 --> 00:27:45,652
(Servant84 becomes absorbed in moving rocks.)

768
00:27:45,653 --> 00:27:46,853
- He is good. - Yes.

769
00:27:47,563 --> 00:27:49,223
Piling up rocks for the stone wall was fun.

770
00:27:49,393 --> 00:27:50,962
It was like assembling the building blocks.

771
00:27:50,963 --> 00:27:53,532
The pieces come together when you don't expect them to.

